ornate.] '''1.''' To beautify or decorate; increase or lend beauty or attractiveness to, as by dress or ornaments; hence, in general, to render pleasing, or more pleasing or attractive; embellish.
{{smaller block|A bride ''adorneth'' herself with her jewels. {{right|Isa. lxi. 10.}}
<poem>Virtue ''adorn’d'' his mind, triumph his brow.</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Fame’s Memorial.}}
<poem>He left the name at which the world grew pale,
To point a moral, or ''adorn'' a tale.</poem>
{{right|''Johnson'', Van. of Hum. Wishes, l. 222.}}}}
'''2.''' To display the beauty or excellence of: as, to “''adorn'' the doctrine of God,” Tit. ii. 10.
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Adorn, Ornament, Decorate, Embellish, Beautify, Deck, Array'', grace, garnish, bedeck, set off. (See ''decorate''.) The italicized words, except ''deck'' and ''array'', are expressive of the attempt to add or increase beauty. ''Adorn'' has the most nobleness and spirituality; it is the least external. Garments that ''adorn'' a woman seem a part of her personality and bring out her comeliness; many virtues ''adorn'' his character; the hall was ''adorned'' with the portraits of their ancestors. In these examples, no other word in the list is high enough or near enough to take the place of ''adorn''. ''Ornament'' and ''decorate'' express the addition of something external, which still preserves its separate character and may perhaps be easily removed. ''Ornament'', as kindred to ''adorn'', is nearer to its meaning; ''decorate'' expresses that which is more showy: ''ornamented'' with pictures; the bare walls were ''decorated'' for the occasion with flags and wreaths. Both express the adding of beauty to that which was deficient in it before. ''Embellish'' implies previous beauty, to which luster or brilliancy is added by something which perhaps becomes a part of the original: as, a book ''embellished'' with plates; a style ''embellished'' with figures of speech. The word is sometimes used of over-ornamentation. ''Beautify'' is the most direct in its}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller|expression of the general idea. Of the first five words, ''decorate'' is the least often used figuratively; ''decorated'' speech is speech in which the ornaments have no vital connection or harmony with the thought, so that they seem merely ornamental. ''Deck'' is to cover, and hence to cover in a way to please the eye: as, ''decked'' with flowers. ''Array'' is used especially of covering with splendid dress, the meaning being extended from persons to animals, etc.: the fields were ''arrayed'' in green.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>But that which fairest is, but few behold,
Her mind ''adornd'' with vertues manifold.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Sonnets, xv.}}}}
{{smaller block|A whimsical fashion now prevailed among the ladies, of strangely ''ornamenting'' their faces with abundance of black patches cut into grotesque forms.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Curios. of Lit., I. 311.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ivy climbs the crumbling hall
To ''decorate'' decay. {{right|''Bailey'', Festus.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|We are to dignify to each other the daily needs and offices of man’s life, and ''embellish'' it by courage, wisdom, and unity. {{right|''Emerson'', Friendship.}}}}
{{smaller block|Nature has laid out all her art in ''beautifying'' the face.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 98.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And, with new life from sun and kindly showers,
With beauty ''deck'' the meadow and the hill.</poem>
{{right|''Jones Very'', Poems, p. 90.}}}}
{{smaller block|Even Solomon in all his glory was not ''arrayed'' like one of these [lilies]. {{right|Mat. vi. 29.}}}}

'''adorn†''' (a̤-dôrn′), ''n.'' [= It. Sp. ''adorno'', ornament; from the verb.] Ornament.
{{smaller block|<poem>Her brest all naked, as nett yvory
Without ''adorne'' of gold or silver bright.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. xii. 20.}}}}

'''adorn†''' (a̤-dôrn′), ''a.'' [< It. ''adorno'', short form of ''adornato'' (= Sp. Pg. ''adornado''), pp. of ''adornare'', < L. ''adornare'': see ''adornate'', ''adorn'', ''v.''] Adorned; decorated.
{{smaller block|Made so ''adorn'' for thy delight. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., viii. 576.}}}}

